From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Thu, 28 May 2026 22:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] freedreno: bounded tiles per submit (env-gated FD_BOUNDED_TILES)
 for A22X glmark2 desktop:blur hang fix

Captured RD of glmark2 desktop:blur w=4 (mesa 0029 patch removed the
FD_RD_DUMP fence-wait so the hanging submit could be dumped) showed mesa
packing the entire frame's work (4 windows x scene + blur + composite)
into ONE submit with 321 DRAW_INDX + 642 IM_LOAD + 150 EDRAM_COPY
resolves. At MAX GPU clock this batch genuinely wedges the a220 CP:

  RBBM_STATUS at hang: 0xc4010310 (VGT + RB + CP + GUI all busy)
  a2xx_progress() reports NO PROGRESS (RB quad counter frozen)
  Real GPU pipeline deadlock, not slow rendering

16 hangcheck recoveries per 3-second blur run = "1 fps" measurement is
1 recovery per second, not real rendering. Subsequent glmark2 scenes
inherit the degraded GPU state (12-37x slower than standalone). This is
the "state accumulation" symptom across the full suite.

memory/reference_kgsl_vs_freedreno_heavy_scene predicted this exact
issue: "The proper fix is to KEEP EDRAM tiling and BOUND the submit/
fence size (split fd_gmem_render_tiles so each submit covers few tiles,
each <2s) -- the KGSL model."

Implementation: env var FD_BOUNDED_TILES (default 0 = disabled). When
set, render_tiles() inserts a sub-submit boundary after every N tiles:
flushes the current batch->gmem ring, allocates a fresh
batch->submit + ringbuffers, re-emits emit_tile_init to restore state,
continues the loop. Each sub-submit gets the kernel's per-submit
CP_SET_SHADER_BASES shader-space-reuse workaround re-kicked naturally,
AND each sub-submit's fence is independently bounded by hangcheck.

The user's draw IB (batch->draw) holds its own refcount on its BO so it
survives the old submit's flush; the new submit attaches the BO on
first emit_ib via the existing reloc path.

Default behavior unchanged when FD_BOUNDED_TILES is unset.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c | XX +++++++
 1 file changed

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
index 9f7124dfdee..6dadfe907ed 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
@@ -590,10 +590,40 @@ found:
  * GMEM render pass
  */
 
+/*
+ * A22X bounded-submission helper: returns the configured tiles-per-submit
+ * cap, or 0 if disabled (default). Env-gated via FD_BOUNDED_TILES so the
+ * default codepath is byte-identical to upstream.
+ *
+ * When set, render_tiles() inserts a sub-submit boundary after every N
+ * tiles: closes the current batch->gmem ring with emit_tile_fini, flushes
+ * it via fd_submit_flush, then allocates a fresh batch->submit + ringbuffers
+ * and re-emits emit_tile_init to restore state before continuing the loop.
+ *
+ * Motivation: glmark2 desktop:blur w=4 packs 150+ tile resolves into one
+ * submit; at MAX clock the resulting batch genuinely wedges the a220 CP
+ * mid-execution (RBBM_STATUS=0xc4010310, a2xx_progress reports no-progress).
+ * Splitting into smaller submits keeps each individual fence within the
+ * hangcheck timeout AND naturally re-kicks the kernel's per-submit
+ * CP_SET_SHADER_BASES shader-space-reuse workaround.
+ */
+static unsigned
+bounded_tiles_per_submit(void)
+{
+   static int cached = -1;
+   if (cached < 0) {
+      const char *e = getenv("FD_BOUNDED_TILES");
+      cached = (e && atoi(e) > 0) ? atoi(e) : 0;
+   }
+   return (unsigned)cached;
+}
+
 static void
 render_tiles(struct fd_batch *batch, struct fd_gmem_stateobj *gmem) assert_dt
 {
    struct fd_context *ctx = batch->ctx;
+   const unsigned nbins = gmem->nbins_x * gmem->nbins_y;
+   const unsigned bound = bounded_tiles_per_submit();
    int i;
 
    simple_mtx_lock(&ctx->gmem_lock);
@@ -603,7 +633,7 @@ render_tiles(struct fd_batch *batch, struct fd_gmem_stateobj *gmem) assert_dt
    if (batch->restore)
       ctx->stats.batch_restore++;
 
-   for (i = 0; i < (gmem->nbins_x * gmem->nbins_y); i++) {
+   for (i = 0; i < (int)nbins; i++) {
       struct fd_tile *tile = &gmem->tile[i];
 
       trace_start_tile(&batch->trace, batch->gmem, tile->bin_h, tile->yoff, tile->bin_w,
@@ -632,6 +662,82 @@ render_tiles(struct fd_batch *batch, struct fd_gmem_stateobj *gmem) assert_dt
 
       /* emit gmem2mem to transfer tile back to system memory: */
       ctx->emit_tile_gmem2mem(batch, tile);
+
+      /*
+       * FD_BOUNDED_TILES: after every N tiles (and provided we have more
+       * tiles to come), flush the current sub-submit and start a fresh one.
+       * The final tail of tiles falls through to the post-loop emit_tile_fini
+       * + the natural batch flush in fd_gmem_render_tiles -> flush_ring.
+       *
+       * NOTE: this codepath relies on batch->draw being safe to reference from
+       * a freshly-created submit via the existing emit_ib reloc path. The user's
+       * draw IB BO survives the old submit's flush because batch->draw holds
+       * its own refcount; the new submit gets the BO attached on first emit_ib.
+       */
+      if (bound && (i + 1) < (int)nbins && ((i + 1) % bound) == 0) {
+         /* Close out the current sub-submit's per-batch tile teardown. */
+         if (ctx->emit_tile_fini)
+            ctx->emit_tile_fini(batch);
+
+         /* Flush the current submit and discard its fence -- only the
+          * FINAL sub-submit's fence is exported to the app via batch->fence
+          * (handled in flush_ring after render_tiles returns). */
+         bool use_fence_fd = false;
+         if (batch->fence)
+            use_fence_fd = batch->fence->use_fence_fd;
+         struct fd_fence *sub_fence =
+            fd_submit_flush(batch->submit, batch->in_fence_fd, use_fence_fd);
+         if (sub_fence)
+            fd_fence_del(sub_fence);
+
+         /* Release non-primary streaming rings explicitly (they have their
+          * own refcounts and would leak if we only del'd the submit).
+          * batch->gmem is the submit's PRIMARY ring -- fd_submit_del handles
+          * its release internally; do NOT del it manually here or we'd
+          * double-free. */
+         if (batch->tile_store) {
+            fd_ringbuffer_del(batch->tile_store);
+            batch->tile_store = NULL;
+         }
+         if (batch->prologue) {
+            fd_ringbuffer_del(batch->prologue);
+            batch->prologue = NULL;
+         }
+
+         /* Drop the old submit (releases primary ring + drops references
+          * the submit was holding on attached BOs). batch->draw lives on
+          * via its own refcount. */
+         fd_submit_del(batch->submit);
+         batch->submit = NULL;
+         batch->gmem = NULL;  /* was del'd as primary by fd_submit_del */
+
+         /* Allocate a fresh submit + primary ring for the next sub-batch. */
+         batch->submit = fd_submit_new(ctx->pipe);
+         batch->gmem = fd_submit_new_ringbuffer(batch->submit, 0x100000,
+                                                FD_RINGBUFFER_PRIMARY |
+                                                FD_RINGBUFFER_GROWABLE);
+
+         /* Re-attach private BOs (matches fd_batch_init alloc behaviour). */
+         for (unsigned p = 0; p < ctx->num_private_bos; p++)
+            fd_ringbuffer_attach_bo(batch->gmem, ctx->private_bos[p]);
+
+         /* Re-attach every resource the batch references -- the user's
+          * draws inside batch->draw have relocs pointing at textures,
+          * vertex buffers, etc. that were attached to the OLD submit
+          * when the draws were emitted. The new submit needs to know
+          * about them too, or the kernel will reject the submit (or
+          * worse, GPU accesses freed/unmapped memory). */
+         set_foreach (batch->resources, entry) {
+            struct fd_resource *rsc = (struct fd_resource *)entry->key;
+            if (rsc->bo)
+               fd_ringbuffer_attach_bo(batch->gmem, rsc->bo);
+         }
+
+         /* Re-emit the per-batch state setup at the start of the new
+          * sub-submit. This rebuilds batch->tile_store + batch->prologue
+          * as needed and restores all the per-batch register state. */
+         ctx->emit_tile_init(batch);
+      }
    }
 
    if (ctx->emit_tile_fini)
